  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to strengthen legislations, policies, and programs to ensure gender equality and equal treatment for men, women, PwDs, PLHIV and other groups in vulnerable situations.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Nigeria, discrimination and violence and harassment including gender-based violence and harassment (GBVH) in the world of work affect women disproportionately, with negative effects on their participation in the workforce and in public life. Women and men with disabilities and those living with HIV/AIDS face similar issues of discrimination in the world of work. They have little access to decent jobs and are often prevented from taking part in fair recruitment processes. They are excluded from training and career opportunities and face negative attitudes from management, human resources, and co-workers in general. Stigma and discrimination against people living with and affected by HIV and AIDS threatens fundamental rights at work, undermining opportunities for people to obtain decent work and sustainable employment. It also creates barriers to access HIV prevention, testing and treatment services: fundamental rights at work are often violated on basis of real or perceived HIV status. Furthermore, the uptake of HIV Counselling and Testing (HCT) is still low among the Nigerian population largely due to poor perception of personal risk and stigma and discrimination: current estimates indicates that over 23% of Nigerians who are HIV positive, do not know their status.
The strategy of the intervention focuses on developing capacity for country counselling ad testing including self-testing to determine the status of workers, policy advice and training. The ILO participates in the Joint UN programme on HIV to review legal instruments and improved protection of the target population.</narrative>
  </description>
